{"id": "2026-09-25-news-france-to-guard-saudi-oil-port-from-houthis", "date": "2026-09-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "France to guard Saudi oil port from Houthis", "summary": "France pledged troops and air defences to protect Saudi Arabia's Yanbu oil terminal as Houthis fired ballistic missiles at the kingdom.", "url": "https://www.euronews.com/2026/09/25/saudi-arabia-reports-fresh-houthi-attacks-as-france-offers-military-support-to-protect-yan", "source_domain": "euronews.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "economy"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "France said on Thursday 24 September it would send troops and air defences to protect Saudi Arabia's Yanbu oil terminal, as Houthi forces fired a fresh barrage of ballistic missiles at the kingdom. President Emmanuel Macron told French television that Paris planned to \"send military assets, meaning soldiers, radars and defence systems to protect\" Yanbu — \"not to engage in any conflicts but to protect this site.\" Yanbu is Saudi Arabia's main crude-export terminal on the Red Sea, fed by the East-West pipeline (Petroline — a cross-country line that can carry several million barrels a day, letting oil bypass the Strait of Hormuz). Saudi air defences intercepted Houthi ballistic missiles after warnings were issued for several regions including Mecca, and the Houthis — the Iran-aligned movement that controls much of Yemen — claimed strikes late Thursday on Aramco infrastructure at Yanbu and an unnamed site in Riyadh. The escalation came as fighting inside Yemen intensified, with the group reporting dozens of its own fighters killed in ground clashes.", "why": "A Western troop deployment to guard Gulf oil infrastructure marks a sharp escalation, and any sustained threat to Yanbu and Petroline feeds directly into oil prices — the same imported-energy channel the Swiss National Bank flagged a day earlier as its main inflation risk.", "thread_id": "2026-09-25-news-france-to-guard-saudi-oil-port-from-houthis", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-25", "event_date": "2026-09-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "Saudi Arabia", "Houthis", "Yanbu", "Yemen"]}
{"id": "2026-09-25-news-delegates-walk-out-on-netanyahu-at-un", "date": "2026-09-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Delegates walk out on Netanyahu at UN", "summary": "Many delegations left the UN General Assembly as Netanyahu defended strikes on Iran and rejected genocide accusations over Gaza.", "url": "https://news.un.org/feed/view/en/story/2026/09/1168431", "source_domain": "news.un.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Many national delegations walked out of the UN General Assembly hall on Thursday 24 September as Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu took the podium to defend Israel's strikes on Iran and reject accusations of genocide in Gaza. Netanyahu called the strikes on Iran's nuclear facilities a matter of national survival — \"if we hadn't done it, we'd all be dead\" — dismissed the genocide charge as what he called \"the greatest lie of the century,\" and described violence by settlers in the occupied West Bank as the work of a few \"juvenile delinquents.\" Both characterisations are contested: UN bodies and rights groups have documented systematic settler violence, and a UN commission of inquiry earlier concluded Israel had committed acts of genocide in Gaza, a finding Israel rejects. The walkout followed a video address hours earlier by Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas — who was denied a US visa to attend in person — warning that Israeli policies threaten \"the very life and existence of the Palestinian people\" and cautioning against \"another Nakba.\"", "why": "Nearly three years after the 7 October 2023 Hamas attacks, the visible exodus from the hall underscores Israel's growing diplomatic isolation, even as its government frames the military campaign as self-defence.", "thread_id": "2026-09-25-news-delegates-walk-out-on-netanyahu-at-un", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-25", "event_date": "2026-09-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Netanyahu", "Israel", "UN General Assembly", "Mahmoud Abbas", "Gaza"]}
{"id": "2026-09-25-news-ethiopia-tigray-fighting-escalates-toward-war", "date": "2026-09-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Ethiopia-Tigray fighting escalates toward war", "summary": "Fighting between Ethiopia's army and the TPLF escalated, with the military claiming 272 rebels killed, threatening the 2022 peace deal.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/ethiopia-s-military-says-it-killed-hundreds-of-tplf-rebels/a-79419173", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source", "disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Fighting between Ethiopia's federal army and the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) escalated on Thursday 24 September, with the military claiming it had killed 272 rebels, raising fears of a return to full-scale war in the country's north. The clashes first broke out on Wednesday 23 September in the Tigray region and quickly widened; the TPLF's vice-president told AFP \"we are in the situation of full-blown war now,\" while Ethiopia's military said its counter-offensive had inflicted \"heavy personnel and equipment losses\" — a claimed 272 killed and about 260 wounded that could not be independently verified and gave no breakdown of fighters versus civilians. The renewed combat threatens the fragile 2022 Pretoria peace deal that ended a two-year war in which hundreds of thousands died. The African Union and the United Nations have called for restraint and de-escalation.", "why": "a collapse of the Pretoria agreement risks reopening one of the deadliest conflicts of the past decade and could destabilise the wider Horn of Africa. DW, 24.09.2026", "thread_id": "2026-09-25-news-ethiopia-tigray-fighting-escalates-toward-war", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-25", "event_date": "2026-09-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ethiopia", "TPLF", "Tigray", "African Union"]}
{"id": "2026-09-25-news-federal-council-rejects-rent-price-initiative", "date": "2026-09-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Federal Council rejects rent-price initiative", "summary": "The Swiss Federal Council rejected the tenants' rent-price initiative and will draft an indirect counter-proposal on abusive rents.", "url": "https://www.watson.ch/schweiz/bundesrat/729120118-mietpreis-initiative-bundesrat-mit-gegenvorschlag", "source_domain": "watson.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council decided on Friday 25 September to reject the tenants' \"rent-price initiative\" but to draft an indirect counter-proposal setting clearer legal rules against abusive rents. The initiative — submitted on 23 June 2026 by the Swiss tenants' association — would require rents to be checked automatically and regularly and abusive ones to be lowered; a rent counts as abusive when it exceeds actual costs plus a reasonable return. The government called that automatic-control mechanism \"very laborious\" and warned it could cut investment in housing construction without effectively curbing abuse. Instead it acknowledged that today's rules on abusive rents are \"complex and sometimes opaque,\" and said its counter-proposal — not yet drafted — would write clearer, more transparent criteria into law so tenants and landlords can more quickly tell whether a rent is excessive.", "why": "The choice sets up the familiar Swiss fault line between enforceable rent caps, which tenants want, and lighter transparency rules favoured by landlords — and because it is an indirect counter-proposal rather than the initiative itself, any concrete relief for renters would arrive only after the legislative process runs its course.", "thread_id": "2026-09-25-news-federal-council-rejects-rent-price-initiative", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-25", "event_date": "2026-09-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "Switzerland", "Swiss tenants' association"]}
{"id": "2026-09-25-news-swiss-vote-sunday-on-neutrality-food-security", "date": "2026-09-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss vote Sunday on neutrality, food security", "summary": "Switzerland votes on 27 September on the neutrality initiative and the food-security initiative, plus cantonal and communal ballots.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/abstimmungen-und-wahlen-darueber-wird-am-27-september-entschieden", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Switzerland votes on Sunday 27 September on two federal initiatives — the neutrality initiative and the food-security initiative — alongside cantonal and communal ballots. The neutrality initiative (\"Preservation of Swiss neutrality\") would write a stricter definition into the constitution: Switzerland would stay permanently neutral, join no military alliance, and not take part in sanctions against warring states — a direct constraint on the kind of EU-aligned sanctions Bern has adopted over Russia. The food-security initiative would commit the country to raising its self-sufficiency rate to at least 70% through more sustainable domestic production, a shift toward plant-based food, and clean drinking water. Polling by gfs.bern in the run-up showed the neutrality initiative losing support.", "why": "the neutrality vote in particular would bind future foreign-policy choices at a moment when Switzerland's alignment with EU sanctions and its ties to the bloc are already contested at home. SRF, 25.09.2026", "thread_id": "2026-09-25-news-swiss-vote-sunday-on-neutrality-food-security", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-25", "event_date": "2026-09-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "neutrality initiative", "food-security initiative"]}
